 al., *Proteins* 1993, 1.8 Å) uses Ambler class‑A numbering, residues 26–290 (positions 239 and 253 are skipped by convention). Exclusion list:\n\n| Residue | Role |\n|---|---|\n| **Ser70** | nucleophile — explicitly protected per your request |\n| Lys73 | general base / proton shuttle in acylation |\n| Ser130 | proton relay to leaving‑group N |\n| Asn132 | substrate amide recognition |\n| **Glu166** | general base for deacylation (Ω‑loop) |\n| Asn170 | orients the catalytic water with Glu166 |\n| Lys234, Ser235, Arg244 | substrate carboxylate binding |\n| Ala237 | backbone H‑bond to substrate; oxyanion pocket |\n| Met69 | adjacent to Ser70; mutations here give inhibitor‑resistant (IRT) phenotypes |\n| Cys77–Cys123 | TEM‑1's single native disulfide — keep it |\n| Ω‑loop 161–179 | treat the whole loop as off‑limits |\n\nReference point: wild‑type TEM‑1 Tm is ~51–52 °C by DSC/thermal denaturation.\n\n## Tier 1 — validated in TEM‑1 itself, all remote from the active site\n\n**1. M182T — the single best‑supported choice.**\nA natural TEM polymorphism identified as a *global suppressor* (Huang & Palzkill, *PNAS* 1997). It sits at the α/β domain interface, well away from the catalytic pocket, and adds hydrogen bonding there (Wang, Minasov & Shoichet, *JMB* 2002, which reported stabilization of roughly **+2.7 kcal/mol**, corresponding to a Tm gain on the order of **+4 to +5 °C**). It also suppresses misfolding and aggregation (Sideraki, Huang, Palzkill & Gilbert, *PNAS* 2001). Crucially, it is nearly neutral for catalysis — unusual, since most TEM stabilizing mutations cost kcat. This is the mutation to make first regardless of what else you do.\n\n**2. Natural stabilizing polymorphisms, as additive second hits:** **A224V**, **T265M**, **R275L/Q**, **N276D**. These recur in clinical TEM variants and behave as mild suppressors of destabilizing ESBL substitutions. All are surface/peripheral and none contact the catalytic residues. My confidence in the *direction* (stabilizing) is moderate; I do not have reliable per‑mutation ΔTm values for these and would not quote any.\n\n**3. Selection‑derived variants.** Kather, Jakob, Dobbek & Schmid (*JMB* 2008) used Proside (protease‑resistance) selection to raise TEM‑1 folding stability substantially. I remember the result but not the exact mutation list, so treat that paper as the place to harvest additional validated positions rather than taking positions from me.\n\n## Tier 2 — strategies that need computation I cannot run here\n\n- **Ancestral/consensus grafting.** Risso et al. (*JACS* 2013) resurrected Precambrian class‑A β‑lactamases with Tm up to **~35 °C above TEM‑1** while retaining activity. The practical version: build an MSA of class‑A β‑lactamases, reconstruct the ancestral or consensus sequence, and graft back only the positions that fall outside the exclusion list above. This is by far the highest‑ceiling route and it is *why* it works — the active site is conserved in those ancestors, so the thermostability lives in the scaffold, not the catalytic machinery.\n- **Engineered disulfides** beyond the native 77–123 pair, chosen by Cα–Cα distance and χ geometry scanning.\n- **Surface charge‑pair optimization** and **Gly→Ala / Xaa→Pro** substitutions in non‑Ω loops.\n\nEach of these requires a ΔΔG predictor or a disulfide‑scanning tool on the 1BTL coordinates. I have neither available, so I am not going to name specific positions for them — that would be invention.\n\n## Suggested build order and assay\n\n1. M182T alone; confirm Tm by DSC or thermal CD and kcat/KM on nitrocefin or benzylpenicillin.\n2. M182T + A224V + T265M as a combinatorial set (8 variants); stabilizing mutations in TEM are often roughly additive.\n3. Then layer consensus/ancestral positions onto the best background.\n\nOne caveat worth stating plainly: TEM‑1 sits on a stability–activity trade‑off (the central finding of the Shoichet 2002 work). Expect most stabilizing substitutions other than M182T to cost some catalytic efficiency, so measure both Tm *and* kinetics on every variant rather than Tm alone.\n\n## ANSWER\n\n**Primary recommendation: M182T** — a natural TEM polymorphism, remote from the active site, ~+2.7 kcal/mol stabilization and roughly +4–5 °C Tm, with essentially no catalytic penalty (Wang/Minasov/Shoichet *JMB* 2002; Huang & Palzkill *PNAS* 1997). Ser70 and all catalytic residues untouched.\n\n**Additive second hits:** A224V, T265M, R275L/Q, N276D (natural TEM suppressor polymorphisms; direction confident, magnitudes unknown to me).\n\n**Highest‑ceiling route:** ancestral/consensus reconstruction of class‑A β‑lactamase, grafting only non‑active‑site positions — up to ~+35 °C Tm precedent (Risso et al., *JACS* 2013).\n\n**Protect:** Ser70, Lys73, Ser130, Asn132, Glu166, Asn170, Lys234, Ser235, Arg244, Ala237, Met69, the Cys77–Cys123 disulfide, and the whole Ω‑loop (161–179).\n\n*Provenance:* literature knowledge only. I had no tool in this session to fetch 1BTL, compute ΔΔG, or build an MSA, so no number above was calculated by me and none should be treated as a fresh prediction with a confidence score.","ttft_ms":69703,"ttft_stream_ms":1348,"time_to_request_ms":85,"type":"result","duration_ms":97521,"uuid":"a5efd81d-26f5-4979-a945-2d1abfd1f398"}